analysisWe need to read the Java code and output an assert statement corresponding to the comment starting with "@@@". The comment is inside the cardinality() method: 

```java
public int cardinality() {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < wordsInUse; i++)
        sum += Long.bitCount(words[i]);
    return sum;
    // @@@ The number of bits set to true in this BitSet is always greater than or equal to 0.
}
```

We need to produce an assert statement that reflects this natural language assertion: "The number of bits set to true in this BitSet is always greater than or equal to 0."

The method returns int sum, which is the cardinality. So the assertion is that \result >= 0. Since it is always non-negative.

Thus assert statement: `assert \result >= 0;`

Make sure to use correct syntax: <code>assert \result >= 0;</code>

Check rules: Use \result variable to refer to return value. That's all.

